{# Shared Jinja macros for QC report templates.
   Usage: {% from "_macros.html.j2" import subject_metrics %} #}

{# Per-subject quality metrics: one table per channel set, one column per metric, one row
   per member. Tables come from hyper_report.subject_metric_tables.

   The shape and the markup are the subject report's own metrics section, down to the class
   names, so a reader moving between a subject page and a dyad page reads one table twice
   rather than two tables once. `qm-flat` is what drops the cell grid: a row is separated by
   a hairline and a value column by being right-aligned.

   A set is its own measurement rather than a grouping of one, which is why it gets its own
   table: GVTD is an RMS across the channels of its set, and a long retention rate and a
   short one are fractions of different montages. An entry whose `set` is empty is a page
   with nothing to split, and prints unheaded.

   Cells arrive formatted, with their colour class already chosen against the registry. No
   number is written here; see qc.boilerplate.vocabulary. #}
{% macro subject_metrics(tables) -%}
{%- for table in tables %}
{% if table.set %}<h3 class="qm-sub">{{ table.set }} channels</h3>{% endif %}
<div style="overflow-x:auto;">
<table class="qm-table qm-flat">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Member</th>
      {%- for col in table.columns %}
      <th><span class="ql{% if col.key_metric %} ql-key{% endif %}" title="{{ col.summary }}"
          >{{ col.label }}{% if col.key_metric %}<span class="ql-dot" aria-hidden="true">&#9679;</span>{% endif %}</span></th>
      {%- endfor %}
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {%- for row in table.rows %}
    <tr><td>{{ row.member }}</td>
      {%- for cell in row.cells %}<td class="qv {{ cell.cls }}">{{ cell.text }}</td>{% endfor %}
    </tr>
    {%- endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
{%- endfor %}
{%- endmacro %}
